# AWS Cleanup Workflow

- Always explain implementation approach before code changes.
- One module per AWS billing service (ELB, EC2, VPC, S3, CloudFront, KMS, ...).
- Elastic IP and NAT belong to VPC, not EC2. Load balancers belong to ELB, not EC2.
- Mark new modules as `PENDING_SECONDARY_VERIFICATION`.
- Keep each module small. Shared helpers live in `lib/common.sh`.
- Cloud Shell entry is one command: bootstrap.sh runs all modules then leftover verify + report send.
- After cleanup, verify leftovers with all-region Describe APIs (EC2 Global View style). Also try Resource Explorer and Cost Explorer when enabled.
- Do not ask the user to paste Cloud Shell logs. Use `verification/latest_report.json` and optional GitHub issue / webhook / S3 report (counts only on public GitHub).
- IAM: delete access keys first; skip only the current caller identity; skip service-linked roles.
- Route53: keep only apex NS/SOA; disable DNSSEC and Accelerated Recovery; poll then delete.
- KMS: customer keys are scheduled for deletion (7 days), not immediate.
